PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A4,B1,B2}

I think the key distinction is that C10/C7 are right that B2 is legal and does not capture B1, but that does not refute C6’s actual B1 line. The correct continuation after 1.W B1 2.B B2 is not to rely on B2 being illegal or on B1 being captured; it is 3.W A4. After that, the merged black group has liberties A1 and A3 only, with W B1 still occupying B1. If Black answers A1, White plays A3; if Black answers A3, White plays A1. In both cases the last liberty is filled and the whole merged group is captured. So B1 is a forcing kill start.

The earlier bad arguments about B2 being illegal should be discarded, but C6’s A4 and B2 lines are not the same as those bad arguments. They use the same shared-liberty net: A4 removes the outside liberty and forces B2/connection, after which White takes A3/A1; B2 forces B B1, after which White can play A3/A4 and reduce the merged group to one liberty. The objections raised so far attack weaker versions of the pro-B1 case, not the C6-style continuations. The complete set of initiating moves is therefore {A4,B1,B2}.

